{{2019 Australian federal election sidebar}}
The '''2019 Australian federal election''' was held on Saturday 18 May 2019 to elect members of the [[46th Parliament of Australia|46th]] [[Parliament of Australia]]. The election had been called following the dissolution of the [[45th Parliament of Australia|45th Parliament]] as elected at the [[2016 Australian federal election|2016 double dissolution federal election]]. All 151 seats in the [[Australian House of Representatives|House of Representatives]] (lower house) and 40 of the 76 seats in the [[Australian Senate|Senate]] (upper house) were up for election.

The second-term incumbent [[Minority government|minority]] [[Coalition (Australia)|Coalition]] [[Morrison Government|Government]], led by [[Prime Minister of Australia|Prime Minister]] [[Scott Morrison]], won a third three-year term against the [[Australian Labor Party|Labor]] [[Opposition (Australia)|opposition]], led by [[list of Australian Leaders of the Opposition|Opposition Leader]] [[Bill Shorten]]. [[Minor party|Minor parties]] and [[Independent (politician)|independents]] also contested the election, the most popular of which were the [[Australian Greens|Greens]], [[Pauline Hanson's One Nation|One Nation]], and the [[United Australia Party (2013)|United Australia Party]], according to nationwide [[Opinion polling for the 2019 Australian federal election|opinion polls]]. The Greens, [[Centre Alliance]], and [[Katter's Australian Party]] successfully defended one House of Representatives seat each.

Australia enforces [[compulsory voting]] and uses full-preference [[instant-runoff voting]] in [[Single-member district|single-member seats]] for the House of Representatives and optional preferential [[Single transferable vote|single transferable voting]] in the [[proportional representation|proportionally represented]] Senate.<ref name="elecsys">{{cite web |title=Australian electoral systems |url=https://www.aph.gov.au/About_Parliament/Parliamentary_Departments/Parliamentary_Library/pubs/rp/RP0708/08rp05 |website=Parliamentary Library |publisher=Parliament of Australia |accessdate=16 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180916055840/https://www.aph.gov.au/About_Parliament/Parliamentary_Departments/Parliamentary_Library/pubs/rp/RP0708/08rp05 |archive-date=16 September 2018 |dead-url=no }}</ref> The election was administered by the [[Australian Electoral Commission]].

As at 12:00AM AEST on 19 May, [[Antony Green]] and the [[Australian Broadcasting Corporation]] calculated that the Coalition had won at least 74 seats, while Labor had won at least 66, with five seats still undecided.<ref name="ABCresult">{{cite web |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/elections/federal/2019/results |title=Federal Election 2019 Results |work=[[Australian Broadcasting Corporation]] |accessdate=19 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190519015530/https://www.abc.net.au/news/elections/federal/2019/results |archive-date=19 May 2019 |dead-url=no }}</ref> Green said that based on his calculations, there was no politically realistic scenario for Labor to win, and the Coalition had at the very least retained a minority government.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2019-05-18/election-results-2019-vote-count-underway/11126576|title=Election results 2019: Coalition ahead on seats as predicted big swing to Labor falls short|publisher=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|date=19 May 2019|access-date=19 May 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190518234933/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2019-05-18/election-results-2019-vote-count-underway/11126576|archive-date=18 May 2019|dead-url=no}}</ref> On the morning of 20 May, the ABC projected that the Coalition had actually picked up the net two-seat swing it needed for a [[majority government]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2019-05-20/election-coalition-get-77-seats-wentworth-boothby-bass-chisholm/11128652|title=Election 2019: Coalition secures 77 seats as Liberals win Wentworth, Chisholm, Boothby and Bass|publisher=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|date=20 May 2019|access-date=19 May 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190519235400/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2019-05-20/election-coalition-get-77-seats-wentworth-boothby-bass-chisholm/11128652|archive-date=19 May 2019|dead-url=no}}</ref> All results are provisional until the Australian Electoral Commission returns writs, which must occur by 28 June 2019.<ref>AEC calendar[https://www.aec.gov.au/election/] of 2019 Election</ref>

The result was considered an upset as polling had placed the Coalition consistently behind for almost three years. However, the Coalition benefited from a stronger-than-expected showing in [[Queensland]]. The [[Liberal National Party of Queensland|Liberal National Party]], which contests elections in Queensland for the Coalition, benefited from the preference flows of other minor parties. The LNP was projected to win as many as 25 of the state's 30 seats due in part to One Nation and UAP preferences.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2019-05-19/how-labor-lost-queensland-and-gifted-the-coalition-a-third-term/11122998|title=Election 2019: Why Queensland turned its back on Labor and helped Scott Morrison to victory|author1=Allyson Horn|publisher=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|date=19 May 2019|access-date=19 May 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190519092803/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2019-05-19/how-labor-lost-queensland-and-gifted-the-coalition-a-third-term/11122998|archive-date=19 May 2019|dead-url=no}}</ref>

On election night, Morrison declared victory and Shorten conceded defeat and declared his intention to stand down as leader of his party, but to remain in parliament.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-australia-48305001|title=Australian PM celebrates 'miracle' win|date=18 May 2019|publisher=|accessdate=18 May 2019|via=www.bbc.co.uk|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190517230525/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-australia-48305001|archive-date=17 May 2019|dead-url=no}}</ref>

