'''Notes'''

{{note label|banks|1|1}} [[Julia Banks]] was elected as the Liberal member for [[Division of Chisholm|Chisholm]] in 2016, but resigned from the party in November 2018 and sat as an independent. She retired from Chisholm to contest the seat of [[Division of Flinders|Flinders]].

{{note label|notional|2|2}} As a result of the 2018 boundary redistribution, the Victorian Liberal-held seats of [[Division of Corangamite|Corangamite]] and [[Division of Dunkley|Dunkley]] became notionally marginal Labor seats.</noinclude>

===Senate===
By 12:00 noon AEST on 19 May, approximately 50% of Senate votes had been counted. Out of 40 Senate seats up for election, the Coalition had won a minimum of 13, while Labor had won at least 12. The Greens looked likely to retain their entire Senate team by winning six seats, while the only other minor party candidates likely to be elected were former senators [[Malcolm Roberts (politician)|Malcolm Roberts]] for One Nation and [[Jacqui Lambie]]. The Senate [[Crossbencher|crossbench]] looked set to become substantially smaller, with incumbent senators [[Derryn Hinch]], [[Duncan Spender]], [[Peter Georgiou]], [[Brian Burston]], and [[Fraser Anning]], as well as former parliamentarians [[Clive Palmer]] and [[Skye Kakoschke-Moore]], failing in their bids to win Senate seats.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/elections/federal/2019/results/senate |title=Senate Results |work=[[Australian Broadcasting Corporation]] |accessdate=19 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190518202957/https://www.abc.net.au/news/elections/federal/2019/results/senate |archive-date=18 May 2019 |dead-url=no }}</ref>

